#### Comparison of photons to \(\pi^{+}\)-\(\pi^{-}\)-spectra from STAR

Most photons in hadronic interactions come from \(\pi^{0}\)-decays. Therefore the centrality dependence of the photon spectra should be closely related to the one of the \(\pi^{0}\)-spectra. \(\pi^{0}\)-spectra are the same as half of the sum of the \(\pi^{+}-\pi^{-}\)-spectra. Thus photon spectra from \(\pi^{0}\)-decays can be simulated by using the spectral functions \(\pi^{+}-\pi^{-}\)-spectra as input to a GEANT simulation of the decays. The resulting spectra can then be compared to the measured inclusive photon spectra.

Figure 5.14: The \(\pi^{0}\)-spectra shown in figure 5.13 are divided by the fits to the \(\pi^{+}/\pi^{-}\)-spectra for four different centralities (top left: 1, top right: 2, bottom left: 3, bottom right: 4).

In figure 5.5 the R\({}_{\rm CP}\) of inclusive photons is shown. The same quantity can be calculated for the simulated decay photon spectra from \(\pi^{0}\)-mesons using the \(\pi^{+}-\pi^{-}\)-spectra as input. The resulting R\({}_{\rm CP}\) is shown shown for four centralities in figure 5.15. In contrast to the determination of the inclusive photon R\({}_{\rm CP}\) no fit of the peripheral bin was used for the simulated spectra. Rather the histograms for the four centralities were divided by the histogram in the most peripheral bin. Therefore the simulated R\({}_{\rm CP}\) in the peripheral

Figure 5.15: The comparison of the measured inclusive photon R\({}_{\rm CP}\) to the R\({}_{\rm CP}\) of photons from simulated \(\pi^{0}\)-spectra are shown for four different centrality selections in minimum bias Au+Au-collisions at \(\sqrt{\rm{s_{NN}}}=62\)\({\rm{GeV}}\). The solid markers are the measured spectra and the open markers the ones from simulation.

bin is by definition equal to one. A big advantage of this comparison is that some of the systematic uncertainties in each of the two measurements cancel by taking the ratio of central to peripheral spectra. Therefore this comparison is more accurate than the comparison of the spectra.

The plots for the two most central bins show that the magnitude of the suppression in the area above 0.8 \(\mathrm{G}eV/c\) transverse momentum is in agreement. In the \(20-40\%\) centrality bin the measured photon spectra appear to be systematically lower than the simulated ones. Below 0.8 \(\mathrm{G}eV/c\)\(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{CP}}\) in the three central bins is systematically below the expected value from the simulation.

Above a transverse momentum 4 \(\mathrm{G}eV/c\) the contribution of direct photons relative to the decay photons is expected to increases. The suppression of the inclusive photon yield then should no longer be dominated by the meson suppression, but the not suppressed photons from initial hard scattering. Therefore the \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{CP}}\) for the three central bins is expected to come gradually closer to one with rising transverse momentum starting at 4 \(\mathrm{G}eV/c\). However the statistical errors in figure 5.15 are too large to see any significant systematic deviations of the data from the simulation.

### 5.5 Photons from electro-magnetic hadron decays

The main contribution to the inclusive photon spectra in hadronic interactions comes from electro-magnetic decays of hadrons in the final state. The two main sources are the \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-mesons. Contributions from other decays are below 4% as discussed in section 1.3.4. They are left out of this comparison, because this is much less than the systematic error on the inclusive photon spectra measured in this analysis. To get their contribution to the photon spectra, the decay of \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-spectra, was simulated using GEANT. For the \(\pi^{0}\) input distribution a power law fit to the STAR spectra of the charged \(\pi\)-mesons as discussed in section 5.4.3 was used. Because the statistical uncertainties on the \(\pi^{+}\)-\(\pi^{-}\)-spectra are much smaller than on the \(\pi^{0}\)-spectra the fits are much better constrained. As there is no measurement of the \(\eta\)-spectra by STAR, the \(\pi^{+}\)-\(\pi^{-}\)-spectra were scaled using transverse-mass-scaling with an additional factor of 0.45. This has been motivated by a previous measurement of the \(\eta\)-spectra by the PHENIX-collaboration, which used the same scaling factor to determine the \(\eta\)-background in the photon spectra in Au+Au-collisions at \(\sqrt{\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{NN}}}=200\)\(\mathrm{G}eV\)[A\({}^{+}\)05].

The spectra of photons in minimum bias Au+Au collisions at \(\sqrt{\mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{NN}}}=62\)\(\mathrm{G}eV\) are shown in figure 5.16 on the left side. The photons coming from \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-decays are obtained from simulation (blue squares and green triangles). The measured inclusive photon spectrum is shown for comparison (red circles). The shape of the measured spectrum and the spectra from \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-mesons look similar. To get a better comparison between the spectra one can plot the ratio of the measured photon spectra to the sum of the photon spectra from \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-decays, which is shown in figure 5.16 on the right side. This ratio is between 1.1 and 1.2 and does not significantly depend on the transverse momentum above 1 GeV/\(c\). The same ratio is shown for four different centralities in figure 5.17. None of them show a strong transverse momentum dependence above 1 GeV/\(c\) and all are on average close to 1.2.

#### Direct photons

The direct photon spectra are determined by subtracting the decay photon spectra from the inclusive photon spectra. In figures 5.16 and 5.17 the ratios of the inclusive to the decay photon spectra are shown. This ratio is up to about 1.2 at all centralities.

The size of the systematic errors discussed in section 4.6 is about 10% on the inclusive photon spectra due to the geometry correction and another 5% due to the embedding procedure. In addition uncertainties come from the \(\pi^{+}\)-\(\pi^{-}\)-spectra used as reference for the photonic contribution from \(\pi^{0}\)- and \(\eta\)-decays, which themselves have an estimated uncertainty of similar size. They are explained in detail in section 5.4.3. The determination of the \(\eta\)-spectra however is even more difficult, because the relative ratio was assumed to be the same as in the Phenix measurement at \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\)

